Title: AI-Driven Dynamic Escape Planner During Natural Disasters  
Natural disasters such as floods, earthquakes, landslides, cyclones, and wildfires can make 
traditional navigation systems unreliable due to road damage, traffic congestion, and 
changing conditions. This project proposes an AI-driven Dynamic Escape Planner that 
models transportation networks as dynamic weighted graphs, where intersections, 
shelters, and critical facilities are vertices, and roads are weighted edges updated in real 
time using disaster severity, traffic, weather, and road status. The system applies Advanced 
Graph Algorithms to generate safe evacuation routes. BFS/DFS identify accessible paths, 
shortest path algorithms compute the safest routes, connectivity analysis evaluates 
network resilience, and Minimum Spanning Trees (MST) establish efficient emergency 
communication networks. Network Flow (Max-Flow Min-Cut) optimizes evacuation and 
resource movement, while matching algorithms allocate rescue teams and shelters 
efficiently. Vertex coloring manages risk zones and evacuation schedules, Dominating 
Sets determine optimal emergency hub locations, and TSP optimizes rescue vehicle 
routes.An AI prediction module analyzes historical disaster data, weather forecasts, traffic, 
and sensor inputs to predict road closures and continuously update evacuation plans. The 
system also provides interactive visualizations of hazard zones, shelters, and evacuation 
routes, enabling faster decision-making. By combining AI with advanced graph algorithms, 
the proposed solution improves evacuation efficiency, resource allocation, and disaster 
response while demonstrating practical applications of Advanced Graph Algorithms. 